WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.540
Hi, this is my Dify assessment project, GitHub Issue Triage.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:10.540
It helps a maintainer review a public GitHub issue and prepare the next

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:11.340
reply.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:16.300
The main goal is to collect evidence before deciding what caused the
problem.

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:22.790
The project has two tools built with the Dify Plugin SDK: Read public

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:25.630
issue and Find related issues.

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:28.370
They call the real GitHub API.

00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:32.230
A GitHub token is optional for public reads.

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:37.450
When used, it stays in the provider's secret credential field.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:44.500
The client checks the repository format and issue number before making
requests.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:48.960
It uses a fixed API host and request timeouts.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:54.520
It also checks that the repository is public before reading issue content.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:01:03.820
Authentication errors, rate limits, missing resources, and network failures
have separate, useful messages.

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:09.950
Each tool returns status and payload_json as explicit variables.

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:14.810
The workflow reads the issue, checks context, and chooses a branch.

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:19.990
API failures and possible security reports go to a stop path.

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:25.470
Ordinary issues continue to related search and report generation.

00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:29.370
If search fails, the main brief is still returned.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:35.000
Here is the published app running a real issue from the Dify repository,

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:40.700
issue number thirty-six thousand two hundred and eighty-three.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:46.420
These captured screens show the input, execution, and final result.

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:52.663
The Studio trace shows seven successful nodes, a runtime of about two point

00:01:52.683 --> 00:01:55.990
eight seconds, and zero model tokens.

00:01:56.010 --> 00:02:00.130
This version uses simple rules instead of a large language model.

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:06.290
That makes its decisions easy to inspect, but limits language understanding.

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:09.880
The report links to the original issue.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:15.660
In this example, connection refused selects a connectivity checklist.

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:21.660
I would check the target service, host, port, and container network first.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:24.120
This is an investigation plan.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:27.900
The workflow has not confirmed the root cause.

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:33.910
Related issues come from title keyword search in the same repository.

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:39.570
They are candidates for review, not confirmed duplicates.

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:43.790
The suggested reply asks for useful evidence.

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:50.030
It stays a draft: nothing is posted, labeled, or closed automatically.

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:58.360
For an invalid repository, the workflow returns invalid_input and explains
the required format.

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:01.593
It stops without generating a diagnosis.

00:03:01.613 --> 00:03:06.400
This keeps bad input from becoming a misleading report.

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:13.010
This version supports public repositories and reads at most five comments.

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:18.030
Its English keyword rules can miss important context.

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:25.750
Possible security reports skip search and omit issue text from the final
brief,

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:31.230
although earlier tool output can remain in execution logs.

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:37.990
The repository includes the plugin package and the exported workflow YAML.

00:03:38.410 --> 00:03:41.330
The published app is also available.

00:03:41.770 --> 00:03:48.290
AI coding assistance was used; these checks and limits are documented.
